<USER_REQUEST>
What is the definition of whole body oxygen delivery, and how is it calculated?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
Whole body oxygen delivery is calculated as the sum of the oxygen delivery to the tissues of the body. It reflects the body's ability to deliver oxygen to the tissues, which is essential for cellular respiration and survival. The formula is as follows:  
\[ \text{oxygen delivery} = \text{cardiac output} \times \text{oxygen content} \]  
The oxygen content is calculated as:  
\[ \text{oxygen content} = \text{oxygen saturation} \times \text{haemoglobin} + \text{oxygen saturation} \times \text{blood volume} \]  

Thus, the total oxygen delivery is:  
\[ \text{oxygen delivery} = \text{cardiac output} \times \left{(\text{oxygen saturation} \times \text{haemoglobin} + \text{oxygen saturation} \times \text{oxygen content} \right} \]  

This calculation is calculated in units of mL/min.
