What is the role of stefins in inhibiting cysteine peptidases, and which specific cathepsins do they target?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
Stifins are cysteine peptidase inhibitors that inhibit cysteine proteases by binding to their active sites. They are involved in the regulation of protein turnover and cellular processes such as apoptosis, autophagy, and immune responses. The inhibitor protein stifin is a 10-amino acid peptide that binds to the active site of cysteine proteases, preventing them from cleaving their substrates. This binding is highly specific and can be modulated by the cellular environment.  

$\boxed{\text{Stifins inhibit cysteine proteases by binding to their active sites, preventing their proteolytic activity.}}$
