2. **At high Reynolds numbers (NRe > 100):** The pressure drop is proportional to the hydraulic diameter and the square of the kinematic velocity (NRe).  

This variation is captured by the **Wenelli-Tchen equation**, which is widely used to calculate pressure drop in packed bed beds:  
\[
\Delta P = \frac{1.5 \times sqrt(1.2 \times 2.5 \times 1.0 \times pi \nu \sqrt{\rho \rho__p})}}{{ \frac{1.2 \times 2.5 \times 1.0 \pi \nu \sqrt{\rho \rho_p}}{1.5 \times \sqrt{\lambda \nu \sqrt{\rho \rho_p}}} + \frac{1.5 \times \sqrt{\lambda \nu \sinh \lambda \nu}}{\lambda \nu \sqrt{\rho \rho_p}}} \left( \frac{\eta \nu \sqrt{\rho \rho_p}}{1.2 \times 2.5 \times 1.0 \pi \nu \sqrt{\rho \rho_p}} \right) \left[ \frac{1.2 \2.5 \cdot 1.0 \pi \nu \sinh{\lambda \nu}}{\lambda \nu \nu \sqrt{\rho \rho_p}} \right)
