","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction:  
\[
3 \text{ CO} + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow 3 \text{ CO}_2
\]  
At 298 K and at constant pressure, we can use the change in enthalpy (ΔH) and entropy (ΔS) values from the Gibbs free energy equation:  
\[
\Delta G = \Delta H - T \Delta S
\]  
However, since the reaction is exothermic (ΔH < 0), and the reaction is at constant pressure, we can calculate ΔS using the change in entropy of the system:  
\[
\Delta S = \sum_{S_{\text{products}} \text{ (ΔH)} - \sum_{S_{\text{reactants}} \text{ (ΔH)}}
\]  
Given the reaction:  
\[
3 \text{ CO} + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow 3 \text{ CO}_2
