","To calculate the work done, we use the formula:  
\[
W = F \cdot \Delta dx \cdot \cos{\theta}
\]  
where:  
- \( W \) is the work done  
- \( F \) is the force applied  
- \( \Delta dx \) is the distance over which the force is applied  
- \( \theta \) is the angle between the force and the displacement  

In this case:  
- \( F = 60.0 \, \text{N} \)  
- \( \Delta dx = 10.0 \, \text{m} \)  
- \( \theta = 60.0^\circ \)  

Substituting the values into the formula:  
\[
W = 60.0 \, \text{N} \times 10.0 \, \text{m} \times \cos{60.0^\circ}
\]  
Since \( \cos{60.0^\circ} = 0.5 \):  
\[
W = 60.0 \, \text{N} \times 10.0 \, \text{m} \times 0.5 = 360 \, \text{J}
\]  

