\]  
Given the standard Gibbs free energy of formation of water (\(\Delta G^\circ^0 = -237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}\)), we can use the following equation:  
\[
\Delta G = \sum{n \Delta G^\circ^0 \text{ (products}} - \sum{n \Delta G^\circ^text{ (reactants}\))
\]  
Substituting the given values:  
\[
\Delta G = 2 \times (-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}) - 2 \cdot 0 - 1 \cdot 0 = -574.2 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]  
Since the reaction involves 2 moles of hydrogen gas and 1 mole of oxygen gas, the total amount of moles is 3. However, the standard Gibbs free change is given in kJ/mole of product (water). Thus, the change in Gibbs free energy for 2 moles of hydrogen gas reacting with 1 mole of oxygen gas is:  
\[
\Delta G = 2 \times (-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}) = -574.2 \text{ kJ}
\]  
