<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the average radiation dose to the liver, we use the formula:  
\[
\text{Dose (Gy)} = \frac{\text{Energy (Gy)} \times \text{Mass (kg)}}{\text{Mass (kg)}}
\]  
Rearranging the formula to solve for the dose:  
\[
\text{Dose (Gy) = \frac{\text{Energy (Gy)}}{\text{Mass (kg)}}
\]  
Given:  
- Energy (E) = 0.12 J  
- Mass (m) = 1.6 kg  
First, we need to convert the energy from ergs to grays (Gy).  
\[
1 \text{ Gy} = 10^ 4 \text{ J/kg}
\]  
Thus, the energy in grays is:  
\[
\text{Dose (Gy) = \frac{0.12 \text{ J}}{1.6 \text{ kg}} = 0.0755 \text{ Gy}
\]  
The average radiation dose to the liver is \(\boxed{0.0755 \text{ Gy}}\).
