To calculate the recoil velocity of the Earth, we use the law of momentum conservation:  
\[ m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2 = m_1 v_{1_f} + m_2 v_2_f \]  
where \( m_1 \) and \( m_2 \) are the masses of the cannonball and Earth, \( v_1 \) and \( v_2 \) are their initial velocities, and \( v_{1_f} \) and \( v_2_f \) are their final velocities.  

The initial velocity of the Earth \( v_2 \) is 0, as it is stationary. The initial velocity of the cannonball \( v_1 \) is \( -90.0 \, \text{m/s} \) (negative because it is directed away from the Earth). The final velocity of the cannonball \( v_{1_f} = 0 \) (since it is moving away from the Earth). The final velocity of the Earth \( v_{2_f} \) is what we want to find.  

Substituting the given values:  
\[ 1 \times (- -90.0) + 5.97 \times 10^22 \cdot 0 = 1 \cdot 0 + 5.97 \times 10^22 \cdot v_{2_f} \]  
\[ -90.0 + 0 = 0 + 5.97 \times 10^22 \cdot v_{2_f} \]  
\[ -90.0 = 5.97 \times 10^22 \cdot v_{2_f} \]  
\[ v_{2_f} = \frac{90.0}{5.97 \times 10^22} \, \text{m/s} \]  
\[ v_{2_f} \approx 1..3 \times 10^{-21} \, \text{m/s} \]  

Thus, the recoil velocity of the Earth is approximately \( 1.3 \times 10^{-21} \, \text{m/s} \).
