","The work done is given by the change in pressure multiplied by the change in volume:  
\[
\Delta d = P_{\text{initial}} \Delta V
\]  
However, the pressure is not constant during the expansion. Since the open end is open to the atmosphere, the pressure inside the cylinder is equal to the atmospheric pressure \( P_0 \).  
Thus, the work done is:  
\[
W = nRT \frac \Delta V}{V}
\]  
The work done is given by the change in pressure multiplied by the change in volume:  
\[
W = P \Delta V
\]  
Since the piston is raised slowly, the piston is in equilibrium, so the pressure inside the cylinder is equal to the atmospheric pressure \( P_0 \).  
The initial volume is \( V_1 \), and the final volume is \( n V_1 \), so the change in volume is \( \Delta V = n V_1 - V_1 = (n - 1) V_1 \).  
Thus, the work done is:  
\[
W = P_0 \Delta V = P_0 ( n - 1) V_1
\]  
For an ideal gas, the pressure is given by the ideal gas law:  
\[
P = \frac n{n_1} \frac{R T}{V}
\]  
The initial pressure is \( P_0 = \frac{R T}{V_1} \).  
The final pressure is \( P_0 = \frac{R T}{n V_1} \).  
Since the external pressure is constant, the work done is:  
\[
W = P_0 \Delta V = P_0 ( n V_1 - V_1 ) = P_0 ( n - 1) V_1 = \frac{RT}{V_1} ( n - 1) V_1 = ( n - 1) RT
\]  
The work done is:  
\[
W = P_{\text{ext}} \Delta V = P_0 ( n - 1 ) v_1 = \frac{R T}{V_1} ( n - 1 ) V_1 = ( n - 1 ) R T
\]  
Thus, the work done is \( ( n - 1 ) R T \).  

